 Everything's prepped and the path is clear:

  - Fly vault is up (vault42.fly.dev → 200; the grobase 404s are just "no
  root route", the API lives under paths).
  - The 42ctl errors you see (os error 6) are because I'm running it
  without a terminal — it wants to prompt for your vault passphrase and
  there's no TTY here. That's the zero-knowledge guarantee in action: I 
  literally cannot seal your data without your passphrase, and I shouldn't 
  have it. So the final seal-and-upload is one command you run.

  Your notes are exported and ready

  ~/osionos-migration/osionos-notes-export.sql.gz — 540 KB, holding all 294
  pages, 5 workspaces, memberships, and 33 page-configs as a re-importable
  dump.

  Step 1 — push to vault42 (run in your terminal)

  cd ~/Documents/42ctl
  ./42ctl-release note add grobase/full-export \
    --project osionos-notes \
    --file ~/osionos-migration/osionos-notes-export.sql.gz
  # → it prompts for your vault passphrase, seals locally, uploads to the 
  fly store
  ./42ctl-release note ls --project osionos-notes      # verify it's there
  --project osionos-notes is your fresh namespace, so these never mix with
  other vault data.

  Step 2 — on the other machine

  # get 42ctl there (copy the binary, or build); the config already points 
  at the fly store.
  # carry your identity over — simplest is to copy your keystore:
  #   scp -r ~/.config/42ctl  you@machine-B:~/.config/      (same 
  passphrase unlocks it)
  #   — or — 42ctl keys escrow  (here)  →  42ctl keys recover  (there)

  ./42ctl-release note get grobase/full-export > osionos-export.sql.gz
  gunzip osionos-export.sql.gz
  # with machine-B's grobase up (osionos schema applied):
  docker exec -i mini-baas-postgres psql -U postgres -d postgres <
  osionos-export.sql
  # then set dev.pro.photo's password on B's gotrue and log in

  Things to know

  - The passphrase is the only key. Lose it → the sealed notes are
  unrecoverable (that's the design). Back it up.
  - vault42 is the encrypted transit, not a usable store — machine B needs
  grobase running to re-import and actually open the notes.
  - Metadata (project name, note path, size) is visible to whoever runs the
  fly store; only the content is sealed.

  Want me to set up keys escrow now (so your identity rides to the other
  machine without copying files), or split the export per-workspace into
  separate notes instead of one? Otherwise, run Step 1 and tell me what
  note ls shows — I'll confirm it landed correctly.

